by wachterjohannes at 2026-09-13T18:40:06Z

On 1: the premise doesn't hold, `redactRequest()` already computes both signatures from one `$body` in the same call, so there's no scenario where a freshly written cassette has v1 but not v2. Redact `$body` once before both signature calls and this is a one-liner, no permanent v1/v2 split needed. That also removes your "lesser problem": a freshly redacted cassette verifies fine against itself through v1, since v1 was hashed over the same redacted body that's stored.

The real gap is on the verify side, not the write side: `assertRequestSignatureMatches()` always hashes the raw live body. Once cassettes store redacted bodies, a live request with a real credential won't match through either path. Redacting the live body unconditionally would risk breaking any already-committed cassette that happens to have credential-shaped text in it, so: try the raw live body first, exactly like today, and only redact and retry on a mismatch before falling through to `mismatchMessage()`. No version flag, can't regress an existing cassette since the first attempt is unchanged.

`assertRequestSignatureMatches()` is `private static`, so it'll need the same instance-method treatment you already gave `redactRequest()` to reach the redactor.

On 2: agreed, redact the live body for the message comparison too, that one's just display and carries no correctness risk either way.

